#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055818} :activity/id #uuid "a550baf0-8755-5225-8d5d-6b6a15c4056b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784 :topic/id #uuid "be6c4008-0408-5477-a85c-6b4f4b170261" :topic/math-academy-id 116} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055984 :tutorial/content "To solve a radical equation where the radical is isolated on one side of the equation, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} = 3,\n$$\n\nwe square both sides. Remember that squaring is the opposite of the square root, so $\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2 =x+1,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the equation in the standard way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =9 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(8)+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{x+1}=3$ is $x=8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60edc96d-ba13-5542-b95d-c7e13ced8006" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3418 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "39bf5cfc-fba2-5011-ab69-295924eb9a74" :step/math-academy-id 534127 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054563 :question/id #uuid "18c11d40-eed5-5af6-ade4-70e024af6cd1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5232"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532644}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"} :step/id #uuid "2578baad-7192-5057-8d26-c29368c0cc0a" :step/math-academy-id 534128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055985 :tutorial/content "Some radical equations have no solutions. For example, consider the radical equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3-x} = -2.\n$$\n\nThe square root of a number is always positive or zero. So in the equation above, the right-hand side is negative, while the left-hand side is always positive or zero. Consequently the two sides will never be equal, and there is no real solution.\n\nWe could also determine that there is no solution by using the same process as usual. If we try to solve this equation by squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-x} & =-2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3-x})}^{2} & =(-2)^{2} \\\\ 3-x & =4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, when we plug this result into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-(-1)} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\ne -2\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we *cannot* accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{3-x}=-2$ has no real solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "c14f3a94-fdf5-5c7a-9929-101b5d933468" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3455 :tutorial/title "Radical Equations with No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "1020b050-7a26-5d3e-b149-d1f8f6f2298b" :step/math-academy-id 534131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054564 :question/id #uuid "1d21fa8f-95d6-55b7-916f-b12d00f2f002" :question/math-academy-id "e-5233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234410}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"} :step/id #uuid "89c128ce-cb37-56a9-8caa-d2032717e896" :step/math-academy-id 534132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054565 :question/id #uuid "2c646210-4559-5e42-9feb-05428d1f674c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235439}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"} :step/id #uuid "f51fc950-6c70-5533-80f1-e63c82a706d6" :step/math-academy-id 534135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055941 :tutorial/content "When we want to solve a square root radical equation which has the square root on the left-hand side and a linear function on the right-hand side, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-1}=x,\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2.$ This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{2x-1})}^{2} & ={(x)}^{2} \\\\ 2x-1 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\nHere, we check that the obtained value $x=1$ is actually a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(1)-1} & \\overset{?}{=}(1) \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=1} \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{2x-1}=x$ has the solution $x=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d17df195-3553-5a81-ac34-282046536fa2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14245 :tutorial/title "Equations Containing Additional Linear Terms"} :step/id #uuid "eb43bcad-ca2f-5d32-b84d-3157063c1495" :step/math-academy-id 534138 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054510 :question/id #uuid "4e5af199-faf5-540b-ad83-c3090e761183" :question/math-academy-id "e-21230"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f806240-4d0c-5c85-b338-51b52df8ece9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548068}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Additional Linear Term"} :step/id #uuid "6beca4aa-4e60-5c71-8b50-ff54c97cb605" :step/math-academy-id 534139}] :activity/title "Solving Radical Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054873}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548098} :activity/id #uuid "5c07c81a-6299-5f56-a72a-da105bc2e7c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784 :topic/id #uuid "be6c4008-0408-5477-a85c-6b4f4b170261" :topic/math-academy-id 116} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de55bb9-4e8d-5f06-b11e-556b8e8249ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547408} {:answer/id #uuid "36d4014c-9f06-558b-b2b4-ea6a2f47a4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3,\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547430} {:answer/id #uuid "59872150-fbec-5d48-9fe6-1b9b9e554ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547513} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad116cc-f346-5585-9c55-b508e61fb4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The equation has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547552} {:answer/id #uuid "7e01fe28-888e-5714-9470-03041ce8461b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547552} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de2b104-d32f-5800-a490-24878cd6ff1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1938b4fe-a4da-5df4-945e-a4ba43cb2798" :question/math-academy-id "q-39686" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3x-8} & =x+2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{-3x-8})}^{2} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ -3x-8 & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+(3x+12) & =0 \\\\ x(x+4)+3(x+4) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-4$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-4)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-4+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-4$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=-3$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-3)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-3+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 1 & \\ne -1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-3$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2x.$"} :step/id #uuid "493c7f13-e45f-5f7f-bf76-16d264e435c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11994bd3-02cd-5d54-9e80-6bf4d1f9a271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547353} {:answer/id #uuid "518703a7-6d82-5deb-9823-04f8892b81fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547501} {:answer/id #uuid "8376cf8b-1e0a-59fe-afd5-0011348cb1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547597} {:answer/id #uuid "a89eae67-e29c-52cf-a4df-72219d50f3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547686} {:answer/id #uuid "b80abb0f-80c8-5d96-a7c3-66cc53eb5542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547686} :answer-field/id #uuid "d572b8ad-f569-5f9c-bd00-4b949d1c4e59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e49f42fb-4ed5-5c34-8e9e-f41f753d3cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39754" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x-9} & =x-1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{5x-9})}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ 5x-9 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+10 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & =0 \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=2$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(2)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}2-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=2$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=5$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(5)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}5-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=5$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1$  are $x=2,\\,5.$"} :step/id #uuid "701496b1-5d28-5293-9b1c-d814d6e67a7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2aaa916-a1f2-5a60-9a45-d8d24134d648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052753} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef944fe-b546-5c3c-bb66-e324e8c66a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547348} {:answer/id #uuid "8610145b-97d5-54e0-93c8-3885eba86069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547604} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5ca04f-34a5-587c-b744-86213c713a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547657} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fb8a27-2cb4-5bca-b2c7-af9e02db982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547688} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db1ca0c-24ea-50bb-aa50-3a7d8e46c64f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e98c0c0b-7009-559f-859a-1e49b934344f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39821" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{2-3x}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-3x} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{2-3x})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 2-3x & =4 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-3(-\\frac{2}{3})} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-\\cancel{3}(-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}})} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-(-2)} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2+2} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2-3x}=2$  is $x=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "99997799-e2eb-5bf3-a5f0-77dab9e1f445"}] :activity/title "Solving Radical Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547296}]}
